    <string name="state_on_now">On now</string>
    <!-- BUDGET-EACH 22 state_on_until state_starts_in state_nothing_scheduled
         These are the second line of the app bar's title, under "Bedtime mode".
         Measured on a 359dp screen: the title column runs x147..878, which is
         209dp, the actions starting at 878. Rendered widths there today are
         138.6dp (en), 137.4 (ru) and 141.1 (uk), so about 59dp is spare at a
         two-digit hour. The duration they carry is the dial centre's compact
         form, "12h 45m", and on a single-day schedule it reaches three-digit
         hours - "167h 30m" - because the next bedtime is then a week out.
         Capped at one line in code, so anything past 209dp is not wrapped, it
         is TRUNCATED.
         The old budget was 190dp beside the switch inside the master card, and
         the reason it was tight is worth keeping: the Ukrainian
         "Почнеться через %1$s" exceeded it and wrapped, which made the card grow
         and shrink under the finger dragging the dial. The card is gone and the
         bar is a fixed height, so a wrap can no longer move the page - but it
         would still truncate, which is why the cap stays. -->
    <string name="state_on_until">On now, until %1$s</string>
    <string name="state_starts_in">Starts in %1$s</string>
    <string name="state_nothing_scheduled">Nothing scheduled</string>
    <string name="badge_once">Once</string>

    <!-- Durations. Hours and minutes are separate so a language with more than
         two plural forms can inflect each one. -->
    <!-- The dial centre. Seven glyphs is the worst case ("11h 59m") and the
         numeral is 36sp: keep this compact or it will not clear the centre well. -->
    <string name="dur_compact">%1$dh %2$02dm</string>
    <string name="dur_minutes">%1$dm</string>
    <string name="window_span">From %1$s %2$s to %3$s %4$s</string>
    <string name="window_span_same_day">From %1$s to %2$s %3$s</string>
    <string name="day_today">today</string>
    <string name="day_tomorrow">tomorrow</string>
    <!-- The weekday, in TWO sets, because the two sentences that name one need
         different grammar and only English is indifferent to that.

         day_span_* goes in the window sentence, where the time comes first and
         the day reads as a possessive: "from Wednesday's 22:30". Russian and
         Ukrainian want the genitive there - "С 22:30 среды до 8:30 четверга".

         day_note_* goes in the plan note, where the day is a point in time and
         needs its own preposition: "заработают в 22:30 в среду". The
         preposition is INSIDE the string on purpose - Russian alternates в/во
         by the following sound ("во вторник"), and no format string can do that.

         Neither can come from java.time, which returns the nominative in every
         TextStyle - FULL and FULL_STANDALONE both give "среда", which is how
         "С 22:30 среда до 8:30 четверг" came to ship. today/tomorrow above are
         adverbs, decline for nothing, and serve both sets. -->
    <string name="day_span_monday">Monday</string>
    <string name="day_span_tuesday">Tuesday</string>
    <string name="day_span_wednesday">Wednesday</string>
    <string name="day_span_thursday">Thursday</string>
    <string name="day_span_friday">Friday</string>
    <string name="day_span_saturday">Saturday</string>
    <string name="day_span_sunday">Sunday</string>
    <string name="day_note_monday">Monday</string>
    <string name="day_note_tuesday">Tuesday</string>
    <string name="day_note_wednesday">Wednesday</string>
    <string name="day_note_thursday">Thursday</string>
    <string name="day_note_friday">Friday</string>
    <string name="day_note_saturday">Saturday</string>
    <string name="day_note_sunday">Sunday</string>
    <!-- The weekday, for the two sentences that name one: the window span and
         the plan note. It is a RESOURCE rather than java.time's
         getDisplayName because Russian and Ukrainian decline it and java.time
         offers only the nominative - FULL and FULL_STANDALONE both return
         "среда", so "С 22:30 среда до 8:30 четверг" was what shipped, which is
         not a sentence in either language.

         Both call sites put the TIME first and the day second, and both read
         the day as a possessive there - "from Wednesday's 22:30" - so one
         genitive set serves both. today/tomorrow above are adverbs and do not
         decline, which is why they stay separate and why the bug only ever
         showed for a window more than a day out. -->

